# Read-only sealed-target check

Guix machine-change comparison needs to read the target binding of each completed
LAGOON product without invoking deployment. The existing receiving predicate is
the owner of that binding; consumers must not duplicate it or infer binding from
a store path or a target name. This increment exposes that predicate, subordinate
to Guix#101 and Bare Cedar Fog#765. It does not complete their source admission or
paired comparison stages.

The command is `check-target CHANNELS STORE PROFILE TARGET`. CHANNELS must be
previously authenticated trusted channel code, as for the receiving entrance.
The existing current-pinned-guix evaluates that Scheme file: this operation is
not a sandbox for untrusted code. The caller must establish that the channel file
has no side effects or stdout output. The no-effects and JSON guarantees below
cover LAGOON operations under this prerequisite, not arbitrary Scheme execution.
CHANNELS must match
the complete channel name/commit list of the already running imported Guix.
STORE must be an exact registered, canonical top-level native store directory.
PROFILE is an absolute path equal to the sealed declaration. TARGET is the
existing cold-red-gate or bare-cedar-fog family. Registration is checked before
and after reading; missing, redirected or inconsistent declarations refuse.

Use the same check-receiving-target implementation as receive and target-aware
rollback: canonical regular declaration files, main unit, enterprise host kind,
instance identity, executable/environment binding and the existing CRG supported
role predicate. Preserve its actor-only/worker acceptance. This command does not
change the separate actor-only receiving requirements described in the runbook.

On success, emit one JSON object containing store, profile, target, mainUnit,
hostKind, instanceId and executionRole (null if absent for BCF). These are observed
facts, not an admission certificate. Errors exit nonzero with no success JSON.
Unknown arguments refuse. The command must bypass the profile-mutation/lease
entrance and must never build, call time-machine, create profile directories,
retain roots, install artifacts, activate services, inspect live host health,
remove retained data or mutate the host. The caller holds its output through its
existing build root; concurrent collection can cause refusal.

Implement in the existing Guile driver using its Guix store and JSON suppliers.
Keep the shared predicate and read-only registered-store boundary separate from
CLI dispatch. Existing receive/rollback effects and acceptance stay unchanged.
No new supplier, resolver, release selector or provenance schema is introduced.

Source-owned qualification exercises real declaration files for both targets and
preserved CRG roles; invalid paths, redirected/missing files and inconsistent
profile/identity/unit/environment/role refuse. Test registration loss, unmatched
Guix, exact CLI grammar, JSON output and effect canaries. Guix registration may
be substituted only in these isolated fixtures; fixture success is not a native
store or installed-consumer receipt. Run existing native transaction regression
suites to preserve receiving behavior. No test may activate a real target.

Before Guix can report a machine comparison, it still needs admitted exact source
and registry/lock/runtime provenance for both products, the actual immutable
construction results, original obligations and measured receiving. This command
supplies only the product target readback stage. Source push, tests and JSON
facts cannot stand in for those prerequisites or production receiving.
